<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>7.17.1: Quadratic Expressions in Different Forms</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m02045</md:content-id>
    <md:title>7.17.1: Quadratic Expressions in Different Forms</md:title>
    <md:uuid>c2a605eb-80ad-49fe-a8ba-2f0b47ee672f</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section class="numbered-exercises" id="sect-00001">
      <title>Warm Up</title>
      <para id="para-00001">
        <media alt="Graphs of two parabolas on coordinate plane with unlabeled axes.">
          <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_08_19_02_01.svg" />
        </media>

      </para>
      <para id="para-00002">Here are graphs representing the functions <m:math display="inline">
          <m:mrow>
            <m:mi>f</m:mi>
          </m:mrow>
        </m:math> and <m:math display="inline">
          <m:mrow>
            <m:mi>g</m:mi>
          </m:mrow>
        </m:math> given by <m:math display="inline">
          <m:mrow>
            <m:mi>f</m:mi>
            <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
            <m:mi>x</m:mi>
            <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
            <m:mo>=</m:mo>
            <m:mi>x</m:mi>
            <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
            <m:mi>x</m:mi>
            <m:mo>+</m:mo>
            <m:mn>6</m:mn>
            <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
          </m:mrow>
        </m:math> and <m:math display="inline">
          <m:mrow>
            <m:mi>g</m:mi>
            <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
            <m:mi>x</m:mi>
            <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
            <m:mo>=</m:mo>
            <m:mi>x</m:mi>
            <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
            <m:mi>x</m:mi>
            <m:mo>+</m:mo>
            <m:mn>6</m:mn>
            <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
            <m:mo>+</m:mo>
            <m:mn>4</m:mn>
          </m:mrow>
        </m:math>.</para>

      <exercise id="exer-00001">
        <problem id="prob-00001">
          <para id="para-00003">Which graph represents <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>f</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                <m:mn>6</m:mn>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>? How do you know?</para>
          <para id="para-00004"></para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00001">
          <para id="para-00005">Compare your answer: </para>
          <para id="para-00006">The lower graph (which goes through the origin) is the graph of <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>f</m:mi>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>.</para>
          <list id="list-00001">
            <item>The value of <m:math display="inline">
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mi>g</m:mi>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:math> is always 4 greater than the value of <m:math display="inline">
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mi>f</m:mi>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:math>, no matter which value of <m:math display="inline">
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:math> we choose.</item>
            <item>Rewriting the equation in standard form gives <m:math display="inline">
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mi>f</m:mi>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                  <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                  <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                  <m:msup>
                    <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                    <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                  </m:msup>
                  <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>6</m:mn>
                  <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:math>. This form shows that the <m:math display="inline">
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mi>y</m:mi>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:math>-intercept of the graph representing <m:math display="inline">
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mi>f</m:mi>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:math> is <m:math display="inline">
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>0</m:mn>
                  <m:mo>,</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>0</m:mn>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:math>.</item>
            <item>The expression that defines function <m:math display="inline">
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mi>f</m:mi>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:math> is in factored form. It shows that the two <m:math display="inline">
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:math>-intercepts are <m:math display="inline">
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>0</m:mn>
                  <m:mo>,</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>0</m:mn>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:math> and <m:math display="inline">
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                  <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>6</m:mn>
                  <m:mo>,</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>0</m:mn>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:math>.</item>
          </list>
        </solution>
      </exercise>


      <exercise id="exer-00002">
        <problem id="prob-00002">
          <para id="para-00007">Which graph represents <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>g</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                <m:mn>6</m:mn>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                <m:mn>4</m:mn>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>? How do you know?</para>
          <para id="para-00008"></para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00002">
          <para id="para-00009">Compare your answer: </para>
          <para id="para-00010"><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> The lower graph (which goes through the origin) is the graph of <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>f</m:mi>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math> and the upper one is the graph of <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>g</m:mi>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>.</para>
          <list id="list-00002">
            <item>The value of <m:math display="inline">
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mi>g</m:mi>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:math> is always 4 greater than the value of <m:math display="inline">
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mi>f</m:mi>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:math>, no matter which value of <m:math display="inline">
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:math> we choose.</item>
            <item>Rewriting the equation in standard form gives <m:math display="inline">
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mi>g</m:mi>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                  <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                  <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                  <m:msup>
                    <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                    <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                  </m:msup>
                  <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>6</m:mn>
                  <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                  <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:math>.This form shows that the <m:math display="inline">
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mi>y</m:mi>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:math>-intercept of the graph representing <m:math display="inline">
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mi>g</m:mi>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:math> is <m:math display="inline">
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>0</m:mn>
                  <m:mo>,</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:math>.</item>
          </list>
        </solution>
      </exercise>

      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00003">
        <problem id="prob-00003">
          <para id="para-00011">Where does the graph of <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>f</m:mi>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math> meet the <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>-axis? Explain how you know.</para>

        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00003">
          <para id="para-00012">Compare your answer: </para>
          <para id="para-00013"><m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                <m:mn>6</m:mn>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math> and <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                <m:mn>0</m:mn>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>, because these are the two values of <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math> that make <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                <m:mn>6</m:mn>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>  equal to 0.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
    </section>
  </content>
</document>